WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:11.450
I was lucky enough in the end of my graduate career 

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:14.360
to get to go to my first scientific meeting.

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:19.290
And so I was sitting in the audience and most of the talks were so far over my head,

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:20.380
I couldn't understand them,

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:20.870
and I thought,

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:21.040
you know,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.780
maybe I don't even like science because I was sort of bored out of my mind.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:29.260
And then this man got up, who will become my postdoc advisor,

00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:30.850
and his name was Michael Silverman,

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.040
and he got up and he gave this talk about a glow in the dark bacterium. That 

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.780
what had been discovered was that the bacteria had the special property that it 

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.110
makes bioluminescence,

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.750
so glows in the dark.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:49.390
And what had been shown by Woody Hastings is that the bacterium didn't glow and then when 

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:50.980
it reached a certain cell number,

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:53.070
all the bacteria turned on light together.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.750
Mike Silverman said,

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:55.290
look,

00:00:55.290 --> 00:01:00.050
you could make mutants and just turn the lights off in the room and find bacteria 

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:05.020
that either make light when they shouldn't or don't make light when they should.

00:01:05.030 --> 00:01:08.630
And by tracking that bioluminescence behavior,

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:11.080
you could find the genes and find the components.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:11.610
And I thought,

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:16.690
surely I'm smart enough to turn the lights off in the room and figure out which of these cells are dark and 

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:17.080
bright.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:19.990
And literally he made it sound so easy.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:25.110
And he made it sound so amazing that these bacteria could work in a group that I 

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:29.980
literally ran up to the podium and was just hanging off the podium and said,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.060
"you have to let me be your post doc."

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.150
So when I went to Mike Silverman's lab as a postdoc,

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.350
he had found the gene and the enzyme that makes the signal 

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:41.110
molecule,

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:44.650
and he had found the receptor for the signal molecule,

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.750
and he had found the genes that make light.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:47.840
And so it was simple.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.580
There was an enzyme that made the signal molecule,

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.250
which we call an autoinducer,

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:55.320
there was a receptor for the autoinducer,

00:01:55.330 --> 00:01:57.730
and when the molecule builds up,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:02:01.258
the receptor binds it and then together that complex turns on light.

00:02:02.008 --> 00:02:06.778
Silverman and found that, that's the story I heard at this famous, to me, 

00:02:06.788 --> 00:02:08.578
seminar that he gave.

00:02:08.778 --> 00:02:11.878
And so what we wondered was,

00:02:11.878 --> 00:02:13.418
could there be more to it?

00:02:14.508 --> 00:02:18.678
And so I went there to find out if there was more to it,

00:02:18.688 --> 00:02:23.558
like were there more than just these two components an autoinducer and a 

00:02:23.558 --> 00:02:24.198
receptor.

00:02:24.408 --> 00:02:29.098
And so my project was to work in a new 

00:02:29.108 --> 00:02:30.698
bioluminescent bacterium,

00:02:31.038 --> 00:02:35.858
and what I wanted to do was to mutate the genes for the 

00:02:35.858 --> 00:02:39.618
known components, that he had already found, and see if there was more.

00:02:41.408 --> 00:02:46.298
What I was trying to do was to mutate the bacterial chromosome and look for 

00:02:46.298 --> 00:02:47.778
bacteria that were dark.

00:02:47.968 --> 00:02:51.348
So usually when they're talking, quorum sensing is happening,

00:02:51.368 --> 00:02:52.248
they make light.

00:02:52.808 --> 00:02:56.868
I was going to mutate them and look for dark mutants, bacteria that made no light,

00:02:56.878 --> 00:03:01.848
figuring if I got a mutation in the gene that made the autoinducer or 

00:03:01.848 --> 00:03:05.798
the gene that made the receptor, since one of those would be gone,

00:03:05.968 --> 00:03:07.128
you know would be mutated,

00:03:07.498 --> 00:03:09.078
then the bacteria wouldn't make light.

00:03:09.808 --> 00:03:13.768
And so I did that experiment again and again and again.

00:03:14.108 --> 00:03:18.678
And the only mutations I got were in the actual genes 

00:03:18.678 --> 00:03:21.178
for the luciferase,

00:03:21.178 --> 00:03:25.968
the enzymes that made light. I never got the molecule and I never got the 

00:03:25.968 --> 00:03:26.618
receptor,

00:03:26.628 --> 00:03:30.818
and that didn't make sense because I knew there had to be those genes.

00:03:32.608 --> 00:03:35.768
So I was trained as a biochemist and this was genetics,

00:03:36.538 --> 00:03:38.298
and I'd never had a genetics class.

00:03:39.248 --> 00:03:42.398
And so I had a big 

00:03:44.368 --> 00:03:45.778
crisis of confidence.

00:03:46.068 --> 00:03:47.788
I was doing these genetic experiments,

00:03:47.788 --> 00:03:48.888
and Mike Silverman,

00:03:48.898 --> 00:03:49.168
you know,

00:03:49.168 --> 00:03:51.168
was a great geneticist,

00:03:51.268 --> 00:03:55.968
and he had taught me this genetics I was supposed to like, very rapidly just dispense with the 

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:58.498
known genes and move on to something more interesting.

00:03:58.598 --> 00:04:02.050
And I remember thinking like,

00:04:02.740 --> 00:04:07.650
I'm just not getting this genetics because I could never get the mutants that I 

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:09.170
was tasked to get.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:10.920
And then one day,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:11.960
I actually just,

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:12.610
you know,

00:04:13.130 --> 00:04:17.550
sat down at my desk after I got the same result again,

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:23.370
which was mutations in luciferase, but no receptor,

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:24.720
no autoinducer genes,

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:25.450
and I thought,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:32.270
I actually finally just stopped doing and sat down and 

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:32.580
said,

00:04:32.930 --> 00:04:36.700
why don't you think about it and thought to myself like,

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.250
how could you get that result? And 

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.650
literally in that moment, and it took a long time to get to that moment,

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.090
I thought up the actual answer,

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:49.940
which was,

00:04:49.990 --> 00:04:54.490
if there were multiple autoinducers and multiple receptors,

00:04:54.820 --> 00:04:58.690
and you inactivated one and another system still worked,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:01.340
this experiment could never work.

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:04.180
An experiment that I had been doing for,

00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:04.400
like,

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:06.180
fifty hours a week for a year,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:07.460
right?

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:11.270
And so I didn't know it was right at the time,

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:15.150
but it actually gave me at least another way to think about it,

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:17.650
and I could try a different strategy.

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.190
And like, when I think back to that time,

00:05:20.200 --> 00:05:24.650
I don't understand why it took me so long to come to that idea,

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:25.690
right?

00:05:25.690 --> 00:05:26.470
But it does.

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.080
It's just the process where you're doing these things with your hands and your brain and 

00:05:31.360 --> 00:05:35.950
information is rattling around in your head and you're kind of in this mess of it 

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.100
and you can't see your way through.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.460
And then all of a sudden,

00:05:39.460 --> 00:05:40.900
like in an unpredictable way,

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.000
which is something I love about science,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:44.960
you have these moments of clarity.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:46.700
And I don't know where they come from,

00:05:46.700 --> 00:05:49.690
and I don't know why it was that day and not later or not earlier,

00:05:49.910 --> 00:05:51.050
but it was that day.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.210
And the instant I thought of that idea,

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:58.180
I literally brought a garbage can over and I dumped all my Petri plates into it,

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:04.254
cleared off my bench, and started a new strategy, 

00:06:04.684 --> 00:06:06.574
which ended up to work.

00:06:09.384 --> 00:06:09.764
I thought,

00:06:09.774 --> 00:06:10.264
okay,

00:06:10.624 --> 00:06:14.344
if these bacteria really do make multiple autoinducers, multiple receptors,

00:06:14.354 --> 00:06:16.494
how would I find the genes for them?

00:06:16.494 --> 00:06:16.854
And I thought,

00:06:16.864 --> 00:06:17.424
okay,

00:06:17.594 --> 00:06:22.594
if I knock out one autoinducer and one receptor, but there's another one working, 

00:06:23.084 --> 00:06:24.384
the bacteria won't be dark,

00:06:24.394 --> 00:06:26.814
but maybe they won't be wild type,

00:06:26.814 --> 00:06:27.004
right?

00:06:27.014 --> 00:06:28.594
Maybe they'll be dim.

00:06:28.984 --> 00:06:33.394
They'll make less light because they're kind of their kind of hobbled. You knock out one system,

00:06:33.394 --> 00:06:34.234
the other one's working,

00:06:34.234 --> 00:06:35.294
but two are better.

00:06:35.904 --> 00:06:40.284
And so that I realized, that I had during the original 

00:06:40.354 --> 00:06:45.254
nonworking strategy, there had been on all these 

00:06:45.254 --> 00:06:47.954
Petri plates where I was looking for dark mutants,

00:06:47.954 --> 00:06:52.954
there had been lots of mutants that were dim, that made not zero light, 

00:06:53.224 --> 00:06:54.594
but not bright light.

00:06:55.024 --> 00:06:59.704
I had thrown all of those in the garbage can because they didn't fit my 

00:06:59.704 --> 00:07:00.764
original idea.

00:07:01.154 --> 00:07:04.694
And so I had a hint, this is going to work,

00:07:05.384 --> 00:07:05.684
right?

00:07:05.684 --> 00:07:09.064
And so I just went back and I collected, I made more mutants,

00:07:09.074 --> 00:07:11.844
and I collected up all the dim mutants.

00:07:12.544 --> 00:07:16.294
And when I sequenced the mutations in those 

00:07:16.984 --> 00:07:17.924
genes,

00:07:19.084 --> 00:07:23.774
it was very clear there were genes for multiple autoinducers and multiple 

00:07:23.774 --> 00:07:24.494
receptors.

00:07:24.504 --> 00:07:26.464
And if you knock them all out together,

00:07:26.584 --> 00:07:31.374
then the bacteria had a real problem making bioluminescence.

00:07:31.554 --> 00:07:32.834
But only if you,

00:07:33.884 --> 00:07:34.344
you know,

00:07:34.494 --> 00:07:36.774
made them so they couldn't speak either of their languages.

00:07:37.124 --> 00:07:39.294
That was a really fun time,

00:07:40.084 --> 00:07:40.614
sort of.

00:07:44.084 --> 00:07:49.024
So before me, there was one molecule; that's Mike Silverman's work and Woody Hastings 

00:07:49.024 --> 00:07:49.714
before him.

00:07:49.924 --> 00:07:50.824
Then with me,

00:07:50.944 --> 00:07:51.824
at that moment,

00:07:52.124 --> 00:07:53.534
there were two molecules.

00:07:53.804 --> 00:07:55.754
And so that did two things.

00:07:55.764 --> 00:08:00.584
It said, we actually don't know the number of molecules, right?

00:08:00.594 --> 00:08:03.634
Finding the second one meant it's not one,

00:08:03.634 --> 00:08:05.024
but it didn't mean it was over.

00:08:05.294 --> 00:08:07.594
So that opened up horizons,

00:08:07.914 --> 00:08:12.704
that there could be many molecules involved. That ended up being true much 

00:08:12.704 --> 00:08:13.114
later.

00:08:13.764 --> 00:08:15.954
And the other thing that was really interesting,

00:08:15.964 --> 00:08:19.974
in my view about that finding, was that that made 

00:08:19.974 --> 00:08:22.744
bacteria very much like eukaryotes.

00:08:23.534 --> 00:08:28.064
So we know that higher organisms, you know, take in all kinds of pieces of 

00:08:28.064 --> 00:08:29.464
sensory information,

00:08:29.654 --> 00:08:29.834
you know,

00:08:29.834 --> 00:08:30.704
they decode it,

00:08:30.704 --> 00:08:35.204
they integrate it, and they behave based on many stimuli around them. 

00:08:35.534 --> 00:08:40.484
And the finding of the two molecules meant that could be true in a bacterium 

00:08:40.494 --> 00:08:41.214
as well,

00:08:41.844 --> 00:08:42.054
right?

00:08:42.064 --> 00:08:46.674
And so maybe they're not so different from higher organisms in the 

00:08:46.674 --> 00:08:51.034
sense of this ability to be computer-like and to take in 

00:08:51.194 --> 00:08:56.034
bits of information, right, and to process it, and to 

00:08:56.034 --> 00:09:00.964
have very sophisticated behaviors based on very sophisticated inputs. 

00:09:01.774 --> 00:09:03.114
That ended up being true too.

00:09:05.534 --> 00:09:10.244
And so one kind of eureka moment is just the new idea and I like that because then you're 

00:09:10.254 --> 00:09:14.154
feeling very frustrated and all of a sudden, you have a horizon.

00:09:15.054 --> 00:09:16.554
I love those eureka moments.

00:09:16.904 --> 00:09:21.414
Other of the eureka moments which are really fun, is you're on that path 

00:09:22.104 --> 00:09:26.474
and you find the answer, like when we find a gene or we find a 

00:09:26.474 --> 00:09:27.384
molecule,

00:09:27.584 --> 00:09:28.004
right?

00:09:28.014 --> 00:09:31.574
And that kind of eureka moment is the "I got it."

00:09:31.714 --> 00:09:36.134
Not the "I have the idea," but, "I got the 

00:09:36.144 --> 00:09:40.384
answer." And that eureka moment is also wonderful,

00:09:40.414 --> 00:09:41.654
but it's always followed by,

00:09:41.654 --> 00:09:41.864
well,

00:09:41.864 --> 00:09:44.554
why is it that, and then you're on to your next path,

00:09:44.784 --> 00:09:45.034
right,

00:09:45.034 --> 00:09:45.984
which I think is fun.

00:09:45.984 --> 00:09:47.064
So you're never done,

00:09:47.654 --> 00:09:49.014
and I think that's a lot of fun. 

